    <recordId>2100</recordId>
    <text>storage-efficient representation of decimal data usually
    n decimal digits are represented by 4n bits in computers.
    actually, two bcd digits can be compressed optimally and
    reversibly into 7 bits, and three digits into 10 bits, by a
    very simple algorithm based on the fixed-length combination of
    two variable field-length encodings. in over half of the cases
    the compressed code results from the conventional bcd code by
    simple removal of redundant 0 bits. a long decimal message can
    be subdivided into three-digit blocks, and separately
    compressed; the result differs from the asymptotic minimum
    length by only 0.34 percent. the hardware requirement is small,
    and the mappings can be done manually. january, 1975 chi chen,
    t. ho, i. t. binary-coded decimal digits, decimal data storage
    6.32 6.34 ca750110 jb january 12, 1978 9:42 am</text>
  </DOC>
