    <text>sitar: an interactive text processing system for small
    computers sitar, a low-cost in teractive text handling and text
    analysis system for nontechnical users, is in many ways
    comparable to in teractive bibliographical search and retrieval
    systems, but has several additional features. it is implemented
    on a pdp/11 time-sharing computer invoked by a crt with
    microprogrammed editing functions. it uses a simple command
    language designating a function, a file, and a search template
    consisting of the textual string desired and strings delimiting
    the context in which the hit is to be delivered. extensive
    experience with sitar shows that the combined powers of simple
    commands, string orientation, circular file structure, a crt
    with local memory, and conversational computing produce a
    system much more powerful than the sum of its parts. july, 1977
    schneider, b. r. jr. watts, r. m. information retrieval, text
    editing, minicomputers, crts,time sharing, bibliographic search
    and retrieval, literary analysis, linguistic analysis, command
