    <recordId>10383</recordId>
    <text>multilevel operating systems the basic software for all
    newer computers is built on the well-established need for
    standard operating systems. this implies that all
    applications-no matter how large, complex or time
    consuming-must operate under (or, more precisely, on top of)
    the standard system. large applications require supervisory
    monitors which handle problems similar to those of the
    operating systems, but at a different level. sometimes, still a
    third or even a fourth such level is required or desirable.
    this leads naturally to the concept of multilevel
    systems-similar vertically, but different horizontally. proper
    division of responsibility between levels leads to greater
    efficiency and less logical complexity, while actually
    enhancing capability. march, 1966 orchard-hays, w. ca660310 jb
    march 3, 1978 1:31 pm</text>
  </DOC>
