    <text>a locally-organized parser for spoken input this paper
    describes lpars, a locally-organized parsing system, designed
    for use in a continuous speech recognizer. lpars processes a
    string of phonemes which contains ambiguity and error. the
    system is locally-organized in the sense that it builds local
    parse structures from reliable word candidates recognized
    anywhere in an input utterance. these local structures are used
    as "islands of reliability" to guide the search for more highly
    garbled words which might complete the utterance. november,
    1974 miller, p. l. parsing, speech recognition, speech
    understanding, augmented transition network, local parsing 3.42
