    <text>accommodating standards and identification of programming
    languages the user public wants standardization and reliable
    identification of programming languages and related services.
    one way of achieving these goals illustrated by the methods
    adopted for trac t-64 interactive language, and its related
    family of languages. oppressive rigidity usually associated
    with standardization is avoided by a new accommodation
    technique accessible to the user to allow local variations with
    the language. explicit standardization of the language is
    undertaken at the organizational source of the language. use of
    the organizational trademark (trac) on the published standards,
    and services relying upon them, provides a reliable public
    identification. these methods can be usefully applied to other
    programming languages and computer services. august, 1968
